  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Oxide and peroxides of sodium are stable (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Rb</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Cs</mi></math> form superoxides. Beryllium forms a hydride with molecular formula&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BeH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>Organic lead (tetraethyl lead; TEL) is used as an antiknock agent in gasoline and jet fuels. The compound&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>BeF</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;on thermal decomposition produces&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BeF</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></math>.</p></blockquote>"
